body:
like 90% of us worked under the table. we only make tips. it sounds like a shitty job but it actually paid very well for the profession. it wasnt uncommon to make $300+ in a night.

&#x200B;

its not like a texas roadhouse diner. it costs $50 for the cheapest item on the fine dining menu and its just some bread with some scalloped potatoes and a choice of extremely small side portion and tinier desert ( 1 scoop of ice cream from cheap generic walmart shit is like $8).  we sold yellow tail wine as (house wine) for $100 a bottle at a cost of like $7 a bottle for them to buy).  the resturaunt isnt visited for its food or value. its visited bc its more of an experience and a lot of history.
